        <div id="how-franklin-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Franklin's Multi-Sensor Design Works
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every cheap electronic stud finder works the same way. One or two capacitive sensor plates measure the density of the wall beneath them, and the tool calibrates to whatever it is sitting on when you press the button. You then slide it sideways, and when the density rises past a threshold, it beeps and tells you that you have reached an edge. That approach has two weaknesses that cause most stud-finder frustration. If you happen to calibrate over a stud, the tool thinks the stud is empty wall and misreads everything after. And it only ever tells you about an edge, so finding the centre means scanning from both sides and splitting the difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            Franklin Sensors replaced that single plate with an array. The ProSensor M210, T13 and 710+ have 13 sensors laid out across the width of the tool, the M150 has 9 and the M50 has 5. Each sensor reads the density beneath it at the same time, and a row of LEDs above the sensors lights up wherever something dense is behind the wall. Place it flat on the wall and the lights show you a band: the full width of the stud, from edge to edge. On the 710+, M210 and M150 listings, the middle LED turns green when the tool is centred on a stud.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because the tool compares sensors against each other rather than against a calibration reading, there is no calibration step. Franklin's listings make a point of this: start anywhere, even over a stud. That is the single biggest practical advantage over an edge finder, because it removes the user error that produces most bad readings. It also means the tool can show you a double stud, two studs side by side at a window opening or wall junction, as a wider band, where an edge finder would report one stud and leave you guessing.
          </p>

          <p>
            More sensors means a wider window, not more accuracy at a single point. A 13-sensor Franklin can show you a whole stud and sometimes the edge of the next one in a single placement. A 5-sensor M50 shows a narrower slice, so you slide it more, and a wide or doubled stud can fill the window. The underlying detection is the same density sensing. If you want the wider market, including magnetic and edge-finding tools from other brands, our <a href="/best-stud-finder/">best stud finder guide</a> compares them. This page is about choosing within the Franklin range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Current Franklin Lineup, Decoded
          </h2>

          <p>
            Franklin's naming is confusing because the same tool can be sold under two names. The M-series models are sold at Home Depot and on Amazon, and several have Lowe's twins in an X-series: the Amazon listing for the M150 calls it the M150/X990, and Pro Tool Reviews tested the M210 alongside its Lowe's version, the X2100. Franklin's materials page groups the 13-sensor M210, X2100, T13, 710+ and original 710 together throughout, which tells you they share a sensor array. If you see an X-number at Lowe's, match it to its M-series twin by sensor count and depth before comparing prices.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 710 and 710+ cause the most confusion. Both are 13-sensor tools with the same core design. The 710+ listing adds a built-in bubble level and integrated ruler and lists deep detection of up to 1.6 inches. Original 710 listings are still around, but the plus is the current version, and it is the one to buy if you want this body style. The T13 is a separate 13-sensor model with a ruler, level and pencil caddy and a listed depth of 1.7 inches. The M210 is the newest of the 13-sensor tools on the market and the only one of them with a live wire indicator.
          </p>

          <p>
            The T6 and T9 are Franklin's smaller T-series tools, with six and nine sensors. When we checked Amazon, the T6 appeared only in a bundle with Franklin's light level rather than on its own, so it is not one of our picks, though it is sold separately at other retailers. The M10 is a 3-sensor centre finder that Franklin's materials page notes is the one model requiring calibration. The M70 and M90 are 7 and 9-sensor tools without wire detection. Then there is the MAX, a different tool altogether, with larger sensors, two modes and a much deeper rating.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
