        <div id="metal-and-wires" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Metal Studs, Pipes and Live Wires
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every Franklin listing says it finds wood and metal studs, and that is true in the sense that a steel stud is dense and lights up the display just as wood does. What no Franklin does is tell you which one it found. There is no separate metal mode, as some Zircon and Bosch scanners have, and a review of the MAX notes that it cannot distinguish a stud from a metal pipe or wire. A copper supply line or a steel gas pipe running horizontally will show up as a dense object. A vertical one directly beside a stud can make the stud look wider than it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical defence is pattern. Studs run vertically and repeat, usually 16 inches apart on centre and sometimes 24. Scan in several places up and down the wall. If a band appears at chest height but not at knee height, or does not line up with the stud spacing either side, it is something else. Pipes also tend to run near fixtures: below a sink, behind a toilet, up from a water heater. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-find-a-stud-without-a-stud-finder/">finding a stud without a stud finder</a> covers the spacing, outlet and trim-nail checks that confirm what the scanner shows.
          </p>

          <p>
            Live wire detection is available on the M210, M150 and M50. The M210 manual describes it as a live wire meter that lights a yellow indicator when it senses live wiring, and calls it an indicator, not a precise locator. That is the right way to think of it. Non-contact AC detection reads the electric field around an energised conductor, so it only works on circuits that are switched on and drawing voltage, can be confused by shielded or deeply buried cable, and will spread its signal across several inches of wall. A switched-off circuit, or a cable in metal conduit, can be invisible to it.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the wire indicator is useful as an early warning, and no reason to drill blind. Electrical cable in a wood-framed wall is usually stapled to the side of a stud and runs vertically from outlets and switches, or horizontally at outlet height. Avoid drilling directly above or below outlets and switches, keep fixings off the very edge of a stud, and turn off the circuit when in doubt. The 710+, T13 and MAX have no wire detection at all, which is the single most important practical difference between them and the M-series.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="depth" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Depth Ratings and What They Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Franklin publishes a maximum detection depth for each model: 1.5 inches for the M50 and M150, 1.6 inches for the 710+, 1.7 inches for the M210 and T13, and 2.5 inches for the MAX. Those numbers are the maximum distance through a wall surface to the stud face, and they are best read as headroom rather than a working spec. Standard residential drywall is half an inch thick, and 5/8 inch is common on ceilings and in garages. Every Franklin has plenty of depth for a single layer of either.
          </p>

          <p>
            Depth starts to matter when walls stack up. Double drywall in home theatres, fire-rated walls and some renovations is an inch or more. Drywall laid over old plaster, paneling over drywall, or plywood shear wall sheathing behind drywall adds more. Tile over cement board can reach an inch and a half once you count the mortar. At those thicknesses, a 1.5 inch tool is near its limit and the signal from the stud gets weak relative to everything else in the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Franklin's own materials page is the most useful guide here, because it reports results by wall type rather than depth alone. Over a single or double layer of OSB or plywood, it gives the MAX 93 percent success and the 13-sensor M210, T13 and 710+ group 68 percent. On ceilings, the 13-sensor group scores 93 percent, the 9-sensor M150 family 81 percent, and the basic M90 62 percent. Those are Franklin's figures from its own user reporting rather than independent tests, but the pattern is consistent: more sensors helps, and for genuinely thick or layered walls, the MAX's larger sensors and deeper rating are what move the number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two things improve depth performance on any Franklin. Hold the tool completely flat, because an air gap of even a millimetre under part of the sensor array reads as lower density and can hide a stud or invent one. And on the MAX, check you are in the right mode: Mode 1 for single-layer drywall, Mode 2 for layered walls. Franklin's manual lists the wrong mode as the first thing to check when readings are odd.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="plaster-and-tile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Plaster, Lath and Tile: What Owners Report
          </h2>

          <p>
            Plaster and lath walls are where every density-based stud finder struggles, and Franklins are no exception. Lath is a row of horizontal wood strips nailed across the studs, with gaps between them that the plaster keys into. To a density sensor, a lath strip looks a lot like a stud on its side, so a scan can produce bands at stud positions and also bands between them. Plaster thickness also varies by an inch or more across a single wall in an old house, which a sensor reads as a density change.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best independent number we found is from Pro Tool Reviews, which tested stud finders on lath and plaster and reported that the Franklin M210 and its X2100 twin achieved 100 percent on-center detection but a 33 percent false stud rate. That is a fair summary of owner reports too: the Franklins find the real studs, and they also find some things that are not studs. Forum posters with very old houses go further and report false positives and false negatives from every brand on plaster. Franklin's own figures put the 13-sensor M210, T13 and 710+ at 80 percent success on plaster and lath, and the MAX at 89 percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            On plaster, use the pattern, not a single reading. Scan the wall at three heights and mark every band. Real studs line up vertically and repeat at a consistent spacing, often 16 inches but sometimes irregular in houses built before standard framing. Lath bands do not line up as neatly and tend to show as narrower, weaker readings. Then confirm with a small finish nail or a thin drill bit in a spot that will be hidden. The MAX's manual adds an important caveat: some plaster is reinforced with metal mesh, and through that, no Franklin can detect reliably.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tile is the other problem surface. Franklin's materials page says the MAX performs best on large, uniform tile, at 95 percent in its reporting, while the 13-sensor models have some success but are much less reliable. Smaller tiles with more grout lines can produce false positives, because each grout line is a density change. If you are mounting a towel bar or grab bar on a tiled wall, the MAX is the Franklin to use; otherwise, plan on confirming by drilling through a grout joint with a small bit. Franklin is also clear that no electronic stud finder, its own included, works through stucco, exterior siding, brick or concrete.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-franklin" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Which Franklin for Which House
          </h2>

          <p>
            For most houses built since the 1960s, with half-inch drywall on wood studs, the decision is between price and convenience rather than capability. The M210 is the best single tool: 13 sensors, a wide window that often shows two studs, a wire indicator and rechargeable support. If it costs more than you want to spend, the M150 covers the same drywall jobs with a narrower window, and the M50 does it for less again. All three include the live wire indicator, which is worth having in a kitchen or anywhere near outlets.
          </p>

          <p>
            For older houses with plaster and lath, buy a 13-sensor model at minimum, and the MAX if the plaster is thick or you hang things often. Franklin's figures and Pro Tool Reviews both point the same way: more sensors help, and the MAX's Mode 2 is designed for exactly this wall. Pair it with a magnet. A cheap magnetic stud finder that locates the nails holding the lath to the studs is a very good second opinion, because those nails only exist where a stud does.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tiled walls, double drywall, sheathing behind drywall or basement walls finished over furring, the MAX is the only Franklin with meaningfully more depth. For ceilings, any 13-sensor model is a step up from the 9-sensor tools, by Franklin's numbers. For metal-stud commercial or basement framing, every Franklin will find the studs, but none will tell you whether a band is a stud or a pipe, so pay attention to spacing.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if you already own a 710+ or T13, there is no reason to replace it with an M210. The wire indicator is a convenience, not a substitute for knowing where wiring runs. What is worth adding is a cheap AC voltage tester and, for big jobs, a <a href="/best-laser-level/">laser level</a> to transfer a stud line up a wall or across a row of cabinets without scanning every placement. For drilling into the studs you find, our guide to the <a href="/best-cordless-drill-for-home-use/">best cordless drill for home use</a> covers the tools that pair with this one.
          </p>

