        <div id="vs-edge-finders" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Franklin vs Edge-Finding and Magnetic Tools
          </h2>

          <p>
            A Franklin is not the only way to find a stud, and for some people it is not the best. An edge-finding scanner from Zircon or a similar brand costs less and, in skilled hands on clean drywall, finds studs reliably. The difference is in the failure modes. An edge finder fails silently when it calibrates over a stud or a dense patch, and needs two passes to locate a centre. A Franklin cannot be miscalibrated and shows the centre in one placement. For people who hang things a few times a year and do not want to learn a technique, that is worth the extra cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some edge finders and multi-mode wall scanners also do things Franklins do not. Several have a dedicated metal mode that distinguishes ferrous from non-ferrous metal, which tells you whether a band is a steel stud, a nail, or a copper pipe. Others have a stronger AC detection mode. If you work around plumbing or wiring often, a multi-mode scanner may be worth owning alongside or instead of a Franklin. Our <a href="/best-stud-finder/">stud finder guide</a> compares those tools directly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Magnetic stud finders are the cheapest option and often the most trustworthy, because they find the steel screws or nails holding drywall or lath to the stud. There is no inference involved: if the magnet sticks, there is a fastener, and fasteners go into studs. The drawback is speed. You sweep slowly in a grid to find one fastener, then find the next one above it to confirm the line. On plaster, a magnet is often more reliable than any electronic tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            The best setup for most homeowners is a Franklin and a magnet. Use the Franklin to see the whole wall quickly, and the magnet to confirm the one stud you are about to put a heavy TV mount or grab bar into. That combination solves the plaster false positive, the pipe-or-stud question and the doubled-stud width question for the cost of a few dollars more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the 710+ out of habit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 710+ is the famous Franklin, and it is still a good tool, but the M210 matches it on sensors, beats it slightly on listed depth, adds a live wire indicator and accepts rechargeable batteries. Unless the 710+ is cheaper on the day, or you specifically want its ruler-edged body, the M210 is the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a single scan on plaster</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lath strips read like studs to a density sensor, and Pro Tool Reviews found a 33 percent false stud rate on plaster for the M210. Scan at three heights, look for bands that line up vertically at consistent spacing, and confirm with a magnet or a small finish nail before you drill for anything heavy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the wire light as clearance</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The M210 manual calls its wire feature an indicator, not a locator. Non-contact detection only sees energised circuits and can miss shielded or deeply buried cable. No light does not mean no wire. Avoid drilling directly above or below outlets and switches, and turn the circuit off when in doubt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Holding the tool at an angle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Franklins compare sensors across the array, so an air gap under one end reads as lower density and can hide or invent a stud. Press the whole tool flat against the wall, especially on textured or uneven plaster, and scan again if the band looks lopsided or flickers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using rechargeables where they are not supported</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most Franklin listings, including the 710+, T13, M150 and M50, say the tool is not compatible with rechargeable batteries; the M210 is the exception. Lower-voltage rechargeable cells can cause erratic readings or low-battery warnings. Use fresh alkaline cells in the models that require them.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

