        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Franklin stud finder is best?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the ProSensor M210. Its listing gives it 13 sensors, detection up to 1.7 inches, a live wire indicator and support for rechargeable AA batteries, which beats the better-known 710+ on every listed spec. For plaster, tile or layered walls, the ProSensor MAX, with larger sensors and 2.5 inches of depth, is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the Franklin 710+ and M210?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both have 13 sensors and show the full width of a stud with no calibration. The M210 lists slightly deeper detection, 1.7 inches against 1.6, adds a live wire indicator and a pencil caddy, and works with rechargeable batteries. The 710+ has an integrated ruler and no wire detection, and its listing says rechargeables are not supported.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Franklin stud finders detect metal?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They detect metal studs, because steel is dense and lights up the display the same way wood does. They do not have a separate metal mode, so they cannot tell a steel stud from a pipe or a nail plate. Use stud spacing and scanning at several heights to tell a stud from a pipe.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Franklin stud finders work on plaster walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With caveats. Franklin's materials page reports 80 percent success on plaster and lath for the 13-sensor models and 89 percent for the MAX, from its own user reporting. Pro Tool Reviews found the M210 hit every stud centre on plaster but also produced false studs a third of the time, mostly from lath. Confirm readings with a magnet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Franklin stud finders have live wire detection?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the current models we compared, the M210, M150 and M50 include live wire detection. The 710+, T13 and MAX do not. The feature only detects energised wiring and Franklin's manual describes it as an indicator rather than a locator, so it is an early warning rather than proof a spot is safe to drill.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How deep can a Franklin stud finder detect?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Listed maximum depths are 1.5 inches for the M50 and M150, 1.6 inches for the 710+, 1.7 inches for the M210 and T13, and 2.5 inches for the MAX. Standard drywall is half an inch, so every model has plenty of depth for ordinary walls. Depth matters for double drywall, sheathing, plaster and tile over backer board.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do Franklin stud finders need calibration?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, with one exception. The multi-sensor ProSensors compare readings across the array rather than against a starting point, so you can start anywhere, even over a stud. Franklin's materials page notes that the M10, a 3-sensor centre finder, does require calibration. On the MAX, you do need to choose Mode 1 or Mode 2 for the wall type.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
